# Chapter 3 · Radical Feminism

> "Radical" comes from the Latin *radix*, meaning **root**. Radical feminists say that **patriarchy is the root** of women's oppression — older and deeper than class or law — and that it must be pulled up **by the roots**, not trimmed by reform. A 2019 MCQ asked what kind of idea "replacing patriarchy" is. The answer: **a radical idea**. Kate Millett was named as a **radical feminist** in MCQs of 2016 and 2023, and Firestone's *The Dialectic of Sex* in 2016. The school was a short note in 2024.

## 3.1  Where radical feminism came from

Radical feminism was born in the United States in the **late 1960s**. Many young women were active in the **civil-rights movement** and the **anti-Vietnam War "New Left"**. They found that the men of these movements, who spoke of freedom and equality, expected women to make the coffee, type the leaflets and stay silent in meetings.

These women concluded that **male dominance** was a problem of its own, not just a side effect of capitalism or racism. They formed their own groups:

- **New York Radical Women** (==1967==), which organised the protest against the **Miss America** beauty contest in ==1968==. (The famous "bra-burning" never happened; women threw bras, make-up and high heels into a "freedom trash can".)
- **Redstockings** (==1969==), whose manifesto declared: "Women are an oppressed class."

::: define Radical feminism | the school of the root
The view that **patriarchy** — the system of male power over women — is the **most basic and oldest form of oppression**, found in every area of life, above all in the **family, sexuality and the body**. Because it is everywhere, reforming laws is not enough; society and culture must be **transformed at the root**.
-> Key words: patriarchy, sex class, sexual politics, the personal is political, consciousness-raising, bodily autonomy, violence against women.
:::

## 3.2  "The personal is political"

The most famous slogan of the second wave comes from radical feminism.

::: thinker Carol Hanisch | "The Personal Is Political" (essay written 1969, published 1970)
Hanisch defended **consciousness-raising groups**, which some men on the Left dismissed as "therapy". She argued that when women share their "personal problems" — about husbands, housework, sex, abortion, appearance — they discover that these are **not private failings but political problems** shared by all women, with a common cause.
-> Hanisch said the title was given by the editors of *Notes from the Second Year* (==1970==), where the essay appeared.
:::

::: example
A woman in Lahore feels guilty that she is exhausted after her job and the whole housework of a joint family. In a consciousness-raising group she hears ten other women describe the same "personal" problem. She realises it is not her weakness: it is a **social arrangement** in which women carry a double burden. The personal has become political.
:::

**Consciousness-raising** — small groups of women meeting to share experiences — was radical feminism's main method. It turned private complaints into shared political awareness.

## 3.3  The founding thinkers

::: thinker Kate Millett | *Sexual Politics* (1970)
Millett argued that the relationship between the sexes is **political** — a relationship of **power**, in which one group (men) rules another (women). She used the word **patriarchy** for this system and said it works through the family, religion, education, literature and, in the last resort, force. She showed patriarchy even in "great" novels by men such as D. H. Lawrence and Norman Mailer.
-> "Sexual politics" means power relations between the sexes. Millett made "patriarchy" the key concept of radical feminism.
:::

::: thinker Shulamith Firestone | *The Dialectic of Sex: The Case for Feminist Revolution* (1970)
Firestone argued that women's oppression began in **biology** — in the burden of pregnancy, childbirth and nursing, which made women dependent on men. She called women a **"sex class"** and borrowed Marx's method: just as workers must seize the means of production, women must seize control of **reproduction**. She even imagined a future where technology (artificial reproduction) would free women from childbearing.
-> Her ideas were bold and controversial; she stands for **radical-libertarian** feminism.
:::

Other radical thinkers you should know:

- **Robin Morgan** edited *Sisterhood Is Powerful* (==1970==), an influential collection of radical writings.
- **Susan Brownmiller** (*Against Our Will*, ==1975==) argued that **rape** is not a rare crime of passion but a tool by which men keep all women in fear.
- **Mary Daly** (*Gyn/Ecology*, ==1978==) exposed practices harmful to women across cultures and called for a women-centred spirituality.
- **Adrienne Rich** (*Of Woman Born*, ==1976==; "Compulsory Heterosexuality and Lesbian Existence", ==1980==) separated **motherhood as an experience** from **motherhood as an institution** controlled by men.
- **Catharine MacKinnon** (*Sexual Harassment of Working Women*, ==1979==) argued that **sexual harassment is a form of sex discrimination** — an argument later accepted by US courts. With **Andrea Dworkin** she campaigned against pornography as a violation of women's civil rights (==1983==).

## 3.4  Two kinds of radical feminism

Rosemarie Tong's textbook divides radical feminists into two groups, who disagree about sexuality and "femininity".

::: fig compare | Radical-libertarian and radical-cultural feminism.
## Radical-libertarian
- Femininity is a trap made by patriarchy
- Aim: androgyny — free people from rigid gender
- Sexual freedom; control of reproduction
- Firestone; early Millett
## Radical-cultural
- Women's values (care, peace) are better
- Aim: celebrate and revalue the female
- Suspicious of pornography and male sexuality
- Daly; Rich; Dworkin
:::

## 3.5  The core ideas in one table

::: table | Radical feminism on the four questions.
@widths 3.2,10.8
Question | Radical answer
Cause | **Patriarchy** — the oldest, most universal form of oppression; for some, rooted in biology and reproduction
Where | **Everywhere**, but above all the family, marriage, sexuality, the body and violence
Remedy | **Transform** society and culture at the root; end male control of women's bodies and sexuality
Method | Consciousness-raising, direct protest, women-only spaces, shelters and rape-crisis centres, campaigns on violence
:::

::: fig cycle | How patriarchy works in every area of life, according to radical feminists.
@centre Patriarchy: male power over women
The family: men as heads; women's unpaid service
Sexuality: men's needs defined as normal
The body: control over pregnancy and appearance
Violence: rape, battering, harassment keep women afraid
Culture: religion, media and literature teach male rule
The state: laws made by men for men
:::

## 3.6  What radical feminism achieved

Radical feminism changed the world's understanding of **violence against women** more than any other school.

1. **Rape crisis centres** and **women's shelters** — the first modern shelter for battered women opened in Chiswick, London, in ==1971==.
2. **Domestic violence** was redefined from a "private family matter" into a **crime and a public issue**.
3. **Sexual harassment** became a legal wrong (MacKinnon, ==1979==).
4. **Marital rape** was gradually recognised as a crime in many countries.
5. **Bodily autonomy** — women's right to decide about their own bodies — became a central demand.
6. The slogan **"the personal is political"** entered every later school.

::: pakistan | Radical insights in Pakistani laws and activism
- The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) fought the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==), which treated rape victims unable to prove rape as possible adulteresses — a radical-feminist concern with state control of women's bodies.
- Laws on **harassment** (==2010==), **acid crime** (==2011==), **honour killing** (==2016==) and **rape** (==2021==) all treat violence against women as a public crime, not a private matter.
- The **Aurat March** (first held on ==8 March 2018==) used the slogan ***Mera Jism Meri Marzi*** ("My body, my choice") — a direct echo of radical feminism's demand for bodily autonomy. The slogan provoked fierce controversy in 2019–2020.
:::

## 3.7  Criticisms of radical feminism

1. **"Women" treated as one group.** By saying all women share one oppression, radical feminism ignored differences of **race, class and nation**. **bell hooks** and **Audre Lorde** argued that it spoke for white, middle-class women.
2. **Biological determinism.** Firestone's claim that oppression begins in biology risks the same essentialism feminists usually reject.
3. **Anti-men image.** Some separatist writing gave critics the chance to call all feminism "man-hating", which hurt the movement's appeal.
4. **Patriarchy as too universal.** If patriarchy is everywhere and always, how does it change? Historians and socialist feminists said it takes different forms in different times and economies.
5. **Division over sexuality.** The "**sex wars**" of the 1980s split feminists over pornography and sex work.
6. **Culture clash.** In societies such as Pakistan, its language on sexuality and the body provokes strong backlash and can be used to discredit the whole women's movement.

::: balance | Radical feminism in a conservative society
Radical feminism's **analysis** of violence and control of women's bodies is powerful and has saved lives through shelters and laws. Its **slogans and style** can alienate the very people whose support is needed. In Pakistan, the most effective campaigns — such as the honour-killing and anti-rape laws — took radical concerns and argued them in the language of the Constitution and of Islam's protection of human dignity.
:::

::: eye | Short note on radical feminism (2024)
Define it (root; patriarchy). Origin (late 1960s, New Left). Three thinkers with books and dates (Millett, Firestone, Hanisch). Two or three core ideas (patriarchy, personal is political, sex class, bodily autonomy). Two achievements (shelters, harassment law). Two criticisms (ignores race and class; essentialism). One Pakistani example (WAF against Hudood; Aurat March slogan). Close with a balanced sentence. A small cycle diagram of patriarchy earns extra credit.
:::
